the video game industry is constantly  growing and generating billions upon  billions of dollars as such major  publishers are always looking at which  games are selling well with an intention  to repeat that success over and over  these kinds of blockbuster games tend to  be the ones that post cutting-edge  graphics be heavy on action and aren't  overly challenging while this isn't  necessarily a bad formula there's  something to be said for the developers  who make games that don't follow these  current trends and instead incorporate  all the mechanics which some may  consider to be dated these are the games  that remind us and play like the titles  you fell in love with in the first place  the game that's been absorbing all of my  personal hours at the moment is divinity  original sin and it's a really good  example of what's so great about this  old-school revival trend original sin  was kick-started for almost a million  bucks and is currently one of the most  talked about rpgs on the Internet as  well as being the best-selling one on  Steam the difference between this and  other popular role-playing games like  Skyrim is that divinity plays like an  old-school computer RPG from 1999 this  means it's hardcore extremely  challenging and easy to make mistakes  that can pretty quickly halt your  progress the amount to which you'll find  yourself struggling in the game is  related much less to which difficulty  you choose and much more to the  decisions you make about your play style  since the game demands some serious  tactical prowess in its combat and  puzzling scenarios a fight can easily be  won or lost by one clever or poor choice  those who think carefully about  optimizing their characters properly  balancing magic users and rogues with  frontline fighters for example will have  a much better chance at coming out alive  oh why is challenging a good thing  because gamers aren't stupid major  publishers aren't usually very keen on  releasing punishing games Skyrim has a  very gradual difficulty curve and the  game scales to your character for  example but the popularity of original  sin Dark Souls and retro platformer is  like shovel Knight  so just that players are happy to have a  challenge so long as that challenge is  fair and can ultimately be overcome with  enough thought skill and practice not  every game needs to be dumbed down some  people think that since games have  evolved so much these older mechanics  should be updated but personally I think  there's so much value in keeping them  around divinity looks gorgeous and feels  modern without letting go of old school  mechanics like turn-based combat  turn-based gameplay was born out of pen  and paper RPGs we're a game master  couldn't very well take on every  player's request to Stabbin zap any  given beasty all at the same time  turn-taking also lent itself to party  driven computer RPGs like might and  magic here you could single-handedly  control a bunch of really different  characters with their own races and  classes executing widely different  attacks or spells at maximum efficiency  these days games are at the point where  they can let the console or computer  process all the player requests at the  same time I have AI help you out with  your party combat like in Bioware's  Dragon Age king's bounty the legend a  reworking of the 1990 strategy game  bearing the same name largely retains  the mechanics its series was built upon  even though they could be considered  dated it's managed to modernize without  completely submitting to the trends of  modern games it's graphically impressive  and has updated certain mechanics like  opting for more flexible exploration but  the dev still understand that a lot of  people continue to appreciate features  like resource management and turn-based  combat fun in the same vein shovel  Knight was created within the graphical  constraints of an 8-bit Nintendo game  and offers old-school challenging  platforming but it implements a  relatively modern checkpoint system  unlike the games that inspired it which  didn't save your progress at all this is  a great and sensible balance of  old-school and new-school given there's  nothing all that sexy about resource  management and slow punishing gameplay  the issue with them that game set on  retaining old-school graphics and  features generally aren't  thought to get big publishers on board  that's the most important thing about  these games that refused to conform to a  streamlined and accessible blockbuster  formula is that they're proving players  are really interested in having these  niche experiences there's a good chance  we'll always have big-budget action  games first-person shooters and hack and  slash Diablo clones but smaller genres  rely on independent publishing and  consumer support Kickstarter has let  gamers have a huge say in what they want  to play and people can vote with their  wallets now more than ever developers  who don't want to compromise on the  old-school inspired experiences they're  offering need an alternative to the  support of big publishers luckily if  things trend the way they are with the  success of divinity original sin shovel  knight and king's bounty it seems that  alternative is fully viable and will  have room to thrive be sure to head to  that comment section and let me know  which modern nostalgia inspired games  are your favorite and which all the  games you don't want to see disappear I  will see you soon
 

